text:
marie: Thinking about a long-term refactor pattern — proposing we name the thin, stable API between gateway, session/cache, and runtime the 'interaction surface triangle'. The goal is to make service boundaries easy to rewire without blasting the rest of the stack.

josef: +1 to the idea of a stable surface. main tradeoff I'm worried about: duplication of light orchestration vs added latency from an extra hop.

ria: Can we keep it low-latency by making the triangle contracts tiny (auth, routing key, kv-hints) and offload heavy logic to sidecar adapters? versioned protobuf + gRPC seems right.

kevin: I sketched a rollout plan this morning: canary a shim adapter behind feature flag, measure p95 delta, then migrate 10% traffic/week. fallback to previous routing if latency > 10% or error rate > 0.2%.

marie: here's a minimal contract prototype — just for discussion:
```proto
syntax = "proto3";
message InteractionHeader {
  string request_id = 1;
  string tenant_id = 2;
  int32 route_hint = 3;
}
service TriangleEdge {
  rpc Forward(InteractionHeader) returns (Status);
}
```
This is intentionally tiny so different runtimes can implement it cheaply.

sophia: Observability question: if we split, we need standardized traces and cost attribution at the triangle boundary. Otherwise RB ops and cost allocation get messy.

marie: Agreed. Add to the RFC: require trace/span propagation, token-level cost tags, and an audit log spec. Also suggest a 3-sprint debt-paydown lane: 1) spec + tests, 2) sidecar shims, 3) rollout + telemetry.

josef: Sounds good. Who owns sprint 1? I can take spec + compatibility harness if someone pairs on the test matrix.

ria: I'll pair on test matrix. Also volunteer to run the performance microbenchmarks. :+1: 

kevin: I'll slot rollout playbook into next cadence and wire up canary gating in CI (feature-flag + smoke test).

eng-bot: deploybot: canary for triangle-shim v0.1 deployed to staging (build 2026.03.22-58).

marie: awesome. I'll draft a short RFC this week (link when ready) with the contract, rollout steps, and observability checklist. Please add any must-have compatibility tests in that doc.

sophia: one more thought — include a short compatibility failure-mode table (silent-degrade vs hard-fail) so product teams can decide expected behavior.

marie: good call. I'll add a matrix for degradation modes. Thanks all — tagging @ria @josef @kevin for follow-ups.
